WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:06.150
The 2004 film National Treasure not only fanned
the flames of conspiracy, it also caused a

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:09.660
large portion of kids to pay more attention
in history class.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.209
But despite its popularity, adults can’t
help but notice these obvious, distracting

00:00:14.209 --> 00:00:15.209
moments.

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:20.180
A crucial part of National Treasure is the
comic relief we get from Riley, the uninterested

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:23.540
computer wiz who just wants to find this treasure
and get rich.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:27.520
But Benjamin Gates won't simply let him do
his thing until they arrive at their destination.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:33.050
"Riley, you're not missing that little windowless
cubicle we found you in, are you?"

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:35.010
"No, no, absolutely not."

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:39.440
Instead, Gates never misses an opportunity
to show off how big of a nerd he is by giving

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.570
Riley a never-ending stream of history lessons.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:46.790
With each new development, Riley is subjected
to a boring lecture about the history of every

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:48.190
brick and stone.

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:51.580
There's even a brief moment where Riley knows
something about history that the rest of the

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:56.040
group doesn't, and thankfully, he soaks up
the moment because immediately after sharing

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:01.350
his knowledge, the dynamic shifts back with
little acknowledgment of his contribution.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.489
Sounds a bit familial, doesn't it?

00:01:03.489 --> 00:01:07.320
Anyone who's ever gone on a road trip with
their dad as a kid remembers stopping at an

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:11.810
empty field to read a plaque and knows exactly
how Riley feels.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:16.200
And it's that history vacation vibe that makes
their dynamic so funny and so relatable to

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.500
the grown-ups watching.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:22.210
Now imagine if each time your family minivan
stopped off at some historic boulder, there

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:25.049
was a group of trigger-happy thugs not far
behind.

00:01:25.049 --> 00:01:29.580
Yeah, it would definitely put a damper on
learning about that 200-year-old pole or that

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:30.580
ancient rock.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:35.340
The first clue given to Benjamin is aboard
a ship called The Charlotte, and in order

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:40.590
to reach its arctic location, he enlists the
help of a mysterious benefactor named Ian.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:44.580
When the two discover that the next clue is
on the back of the Declaration of Independence,

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:49.320
they quickly conclude that the only way to
find out for sure would be to steal it.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.490
However, Benjamin refuses to help Ian steal
the historical document, so the two beef it

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:57.700
out and blow up a ship, which lays out the
good versus evil battle for the rest of the

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.140
two-hour scavenger hunt.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:04.689
Benjamin has dedicated his entire life to
the pursuit of a treasure beyond all imagining.

00:02:04.689 --> 00:02:09.110
It shows nobility that he refuses to endanger
the Declaration of Independence even though

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:11.720
they're closer to achieving his life's pursuit.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:16.290
But then he twists his own logic around and
decides that in order to protect the document,

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:17.290
he must steal the document.

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:18.290
Wait, what?!

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:22.120
"I'm going to steal the Declaration of Independence."

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:26.730
So, rather than just work with Ian, he told
him no and then went off to do the same thing

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:28.310
without his help?

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:30.150
That's pretty inconsiderate.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:34.980
Ian may have had experience in operations
of questionable legality, but at every junction,

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:39.080
he lays out his requests reasonably and is
constantly telling his men to calm it down

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:40.810
with all the shooting.

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:44.410
All the turmoil that developed throughout
the film could've been avoided if Benjamin

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:48.069
had just worked with Ian to do the same thing
he was going to do anyway.

00:02:48.069 --> 00:02:52.110
The Declaration of Independence would've still
been stolen, but no one would've been shot

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:53.110
at.

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:54.630
The FBI wouldn't be on their heels.

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:58.080
Also, as a little bonus, they still would've
found the treasure.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:04.000
Before they commit to stealing the Declaration
of Independence, in true heist fashion, Riley

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.500
lays out the impossibility of it all.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:11.010
The building is a fortress, and there are
sensors, thermal monitoring, a vault surrounded

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:14.260
by loads of concrete — you know, the usual.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:18.590
Considering this is the most important document
in the history of the United States, it would

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:23.360
stand to reason that the entire building would
be airtight, but not quite.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.430
It's nothing that a power tool and a laptop
can't penetrate.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:30.540
And seriously, what's with the room that Riley
just walks into and hooks up to the security

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:31.540
cameras?

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:35.930
Sure, your technological van guy needs eyes
on the facility, but you would think a government

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.380
building would have a few hoops to jump through.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:40.030
But not in National Treasure.

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:41.520
No hoops at all.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.470
Riley waltzes into some random room that's
unlocked and hooks up to the building's security

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.630
cameras through a tube of wires.

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:51.069
Now we're starting to see why being a bank
robber seemed doable as a kid.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:56.700
In National Treasure, the characters spend
a large amount of time explaining how impenetrable

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.970
the security is around the Declaration of
Independence.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:05.099
Gates and Riley are laughed out of both the
FBI and Homeland Security for even suggesting

00:04:05.099 --> 00:04:06.800
that it could be stolen.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:11.060
The quick little CGI demonstration of the
document being lowered into its vault makes

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:12.819
it seem pretty secure.

00:04:12.819 --> 00:04:17.120
Well, except when it's moved to a different
room on the same weekend as a big gala, where

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.099
it's guarded by just one security guy.

00:04:20.099 --> 00:04:24.689
Aside from the coincidental nature of the
party the team uses to mask their heist, adults

00:04:24.689 --> 00:04:29.039
watching National Treasure were also left
wondering why an entire section of the building

00:04:29.039 --> 00:04:30.629
was completely empty.

00:04:30.629 --> 00:04:35.430
A suave, tuxedo-wearing Nicolas Cage saunters
in and then scurries out carrying the whole

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:36.630
case.

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:40.310
There's even a second way to penetrate the
building's defenses, which Ian takes through

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:42.449
a series of sewers and vents.

00:04:42.449 --> 00:04:46.289
By this point in the film, it's apparent that
nothing they're doing is all that difficult.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.379
There's a whole lot of lemon action happening
in National Treasure.

00:04:50.379 --> 00:04:54.900
In addition to the compound that Benjamin
makes to figure out Abigail's password, these

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:59.990
fruits help our heroes decipher a code on
the back of the Declaration of Independence.

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:03.569
After the characters have stolen the document,
they find themselves at Benjamin's father

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:07.099
Patrick's house, examining the historical
parchment.

00:05:07.099 --> 00:05:10.860
And it's a good thing there's a lemon nearby
so they can gingerly swab the document in

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.879
search of a hidden map.

00:05:12.879 --> 00:05:16.069
Once they discover it works, they seek out
more lemons.

00:05:16.069 --> 00:05:19.099
In most households, a lemon or two may be
standard.

00:05:19.099 --> 00:05:23.759
Those couple of lemons may even sit in the
fridge for weeks until their purpose is revealed.

00:05:23.759 --> 00:05:27.389
But history buffs dance to a different tune,
apparently.

00:05:27.389 --> 00:05:31.580
Patrick Gates appears to lead a life of solitude,
complete with pizza and soda for dinner in

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:35.309
his bachelor pad, surrounded by history relics
and books.

00:05:35.309 --> 00:05:39.590
Fellow adults were left wondering why such
a man has a large portion of his fridge space

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:42.460
being taken up by a huge bowl of lemons.

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:47.069
Perhaps the Gates patriarch is fighting off
a kidney stone, or maybe sucking the sour

00:05:47.069 --> 00:05:50.879
citrus down to the rind each morning helps
him feel alive.

00:05:50.879 --> 00:05:54.610
Whatever the reason, the treasure hunters
are lucky he's such a fan of lemons.

00:05:55.809 --> 00:05:59.789
At one point in the film, Benjamin Gates and
his crew are about to head off in search of

00:05:59.789 --> 00:06:04.199
their next clue ... but first, they stop off
to change clothes.

00:06:04.199 --> 00:06:09.669
While changing in separate stalls, Benjamin
and Abigail display a rare moment of chemistry.

00:06:09.669 --> 00:06:13.830
Changing rooms in every store across America
are a private endeavor, complete with personal

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:16.089
lock and his and her sections.

00:06:16.089 --> 00:06:19.659
Even from a progressive standpoint though,
the changing rooms these two are conversing

00:06:19.659 --> 00:06:21.649
in seem a bit revealing.

00:06:21.649 --> 00:06:25.800
The way the shot is set up also makes it appear
as though the entire store is able to look

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.819
in on them changing.

00:06:27.819 --> 00:06:32.379
They're stripping down to their skivvies with
two weird Old West saloon doors barely covering

00:06:32.379 --> 00:06:34.559
them up to about chest height.

00:06:34.559 --> 00:06:38.400
It's certainly a different setup from the
usual Urban Outfitters.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:42.169
While we'd like to believe that it's a quiet
statement about equality and not being ashamed

00:06:42.169 --> 00:06:46.869
of our bodies, it's mostly obvious that the
filmmakers wanted a still frame shot of both

00:06:46.869 --> 00:06:48.270
the stars talking.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:52.229
And while we don't know for sure, we're willing
to guess that Disney laid out exactly how

00:06:52.229 --> 00:06:56.629
much skin could be shown, and they cut their
little saloon doors out to their exact specifications.

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:02.400
When there's an incident like someone stealing
the Declaration of Independence, it stands

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:06.399
to reason that the FBI would be called in
to hunt down the perpetrators.

00:07:06.399 --> 00:07:11.330
After all, the FBI has a vast arsenal of knowledge
and resources at their disposal.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:15.700
The leader of the task force, Agent Peter
Sadusky, has all the characteristics of a

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:17.939
smart and efficient team leader.

00:07:17.939 --> 00:07:21.879
But despite all these attributes, there seems
to be little noise heard about the British

00:07:21.879 --> 00:07:27.379
gunmen chasing Americans across the country,
firing clip after clip around national monuments.

00:07:27.379 --> 00:07:32.270
There are several moments in the movie when
Benjamin and his crew find themselves dodging

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:34.439
a hail of bullets.

00:07:34.439 --> 00:07:37.909
National Treasure filmmakers would have you
believe that the silencers the bad guys have

00:07:37.909 --> 00:07:42.919
on their guns mask all the mayhem, but adults
see right through the illusion.

00:07:42.919 --> 00:07:47.149
Silencers don't completely silence the sound
of a gunshot, and it's hard to ignore a woman

00:07:47.149 --> 00:07:52.649
and two men running for their lives while
being chased by burly men brandishing pistols.

00:07:52.649 --> 00:07:57.550
So ... not a single alert was passed along
to the FBI about any of this?

00:07:57.550 --> 00:07:58.680
Evidently not.

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:03.439
In fact, the thing they seem to be most focused
on is the moment when Benjamin uses his credit

00:08:03.439 --> 00:08:04.789
card after the heist.

00:08:04.789 --> 00:08:07.389
So much for innocent until proven guilty.

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:12.919
The Declaration of Independence spends most
of National Treasure encased in a sturdy tube

00:08:12.919 --> 00:08:14.969
within another sturdy tube.

00:08:14.969 --> 00:08:16.199
It gets blow-dried.

00:08:16.199 --> 00:08:17.780
It gets covered in lemon juice.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:19.279
It gets thrown into traffic.

00:08:19.279 --> 00:08:23.659
But luckily, it doesn't fall a few stories
in a mine shaft, thanks to the quick thinking

00:08:23.659 --> 00:08:28.069
and impeccable timing of the one and only
Nicolas Cage.

00:08:28.069 --> 00:08:32.469
Historical significance aside, there's little
doubt that every adult couple watching National

00:08:32.469 --> 00:08:37.880
Treasure got a sideways glance when Ben dropped
Abigail in order to save the tube of history.

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:41.800
The logical decision would've been to save
the girl and let the document drop down the

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:44.290
shaft safely in its durable casing.

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:47.089
They were all headed down to the bottom anyways.

00:08:47.089 --> 00:08:49.510
Even Ben can't believe he made that choice.

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:53.840
When Abigail says she would've done the same
thing, he stares back at her, bewildered,

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:55.069
and whimpers,

00:08:55.069 --> 00:08:56.120
"Really?"

00:08:56.120 --> 00:09:01.310
The chemistry between the two leads is off,
and it rubs any grown human the wrong way.

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:06.850
The young National Archive employee went from
her posh office to being kidnapped, shot at,

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:11.189
and dropped down a mine shaft, then succumbs
to the charms of a 40-year-old treasure hunter

00:09:11.189 --> 00:09:14.279
... who was the one who dropped her in the
first place.

00:09:14.279 --> 00:09:15.279
Seems reasonable.

00:09:16.650 --> 00:09:21.220
Quick thinking and vast historical knowledge
across two generations leaves the crew stranded

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:25.839
at the bottom of some catacombs but free of
Ian's strong-arm tactics.

00:09:25.839 --> 00:09:31.300
And once our heroes escape back out into fresh
air, Ben contacts the FBI and offers Sadusky

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:33.180
someone to send to jail.

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:39.100
The Freemason Federal agent obliges and sets
off to arrest Ian for a slew of charges.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:43.190
Benjamin Gates tags along because the discovery
of an enormous treasure room isn't enough

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:44.690
for the glory hunter.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:49.410
The historian portrays an urge to rub it in
Ian's face with some drama.

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:54.000
When Sadusky arrests Ian outside the church
he was led to, Ben steps out from the shadows

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:55.899
in a nearby doorway to smile at Ian.

00:09:55.899 --> 00:10:01.351
It stands to reason that all the FBI agents
were positioned nearby, ready to pounce, but

00:10:01.351 --> 00:10:03.120
what was Ben's story there?

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:07.330
The man went from facing a lifetime in prison
to having the FBI let him lurk on someone's

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:08.879
front porch in the shadows.

00:10:08.879 --> 00:10:12.880
There must have been a significant amount
of time that he was waiting there, hiding

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:13.990
from the light.

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:19.300
It does make the ending pretty humorous, picturing
Nicolas Cage hiding in a dark doorway, brimming

00:10:19.300 --> 00:10:24.839
with excitement as he hears the chains on
the nearby church clink against the bolt cutters.

